**Yes.** Since ECMAScript 2019 (version 10), the specification requires `Array.prototype.sort` to be stable. The MDN page states: "Since version 10 (or ECMAScript 2019), the specification dictates that `Array.prototype.sort` is stable." Elements that compare as equal keep their original relative order.

```js
const students = [
  { name: "Alex", grade: 15 },
  { name: "Devlin", grade: 15 },
  { name: "Eagle", grade: 13 },
  { name: "Sam", grade: 14 },
];

students.sort((a, b) => a.grade - b.grade);
// [
//   { name: "Eagle", grade: 13 },
//   { name: "Sam", grade: 14 },
//   { name: "Alex", grade: 15 },   // Alex still precedes Devlin
//   { name: "Devlin", grade: 15 },
// ]
```

Before ECMAScript 2019, engines were not required to preserve this order, so equal-grade items could end up swapped. Code that must run on older engines should not rely on stability.

Yes. Since ECMAScript 2019 (ES10), the specification requires `Array.prototype.sort` to be stable. Elements that compare as equal keep their original relative order.

Before ES2019, stability was not guaranteed, so engines could reorder equal elements.

The students example from the page shows the guarantee. The input is already sorted by name:

```js
const students = [
  { name: "Alex", grade: 15 },
  { name: "Devlin", grade: 15 },
  { name: "Eagle", grade: 13 },
  { name: "Sam", grade: 14 },
];

students.sort((firstItem, secondItem) => firstItem.grade - secondItem.grade);
// [
//   { name: "Eagle", grade: 13 },
//   { name: "Sam", grade: 14 },
//   { name: "Alex", grade: 15 },   // original order maintained (stable)
//   { name: "Devlin", grade: 15 }, // original order maintained (stable)
// ]
```

Stability only holds if your comparator is well-formed. The page warns that a comparator that breaks the required properties (such as anti-symmetry) gives undefined behavior, and engines may disagree on the result.

