When no compare function is passed, `sort()` converts each element to a string and compares the strings by their UTF-16 code unit values. Numbers are therefore sorted **lexicographically (as text), not numerically**. As the page notes, "9 comes before 80, but because numbers are converted to strings, '80' comes before '9'."

```js
const array = [1, 30, 4, 21, 100000];
array.sort();
console.log(array);
// Expected output: Array [1, 100000, 21, 30, 4]

const numberArray = [40, 1, 5, 200];
numberArray.sort(); // [1, 200, 40, 5]
```

To sort numbers numerically, pass a compare function:

```js
function compareNumbers(a, b) {
  return a - b;
}

numberArray.sort(compareNumbers); // [1, 5, 40, 200]
```

When no compare function is passed, `sort()` **converts the numbers to strings and compares them by UTF-16 code unit values**, so the order is lexicographic (dictionary-style), not numeric. As the MDN page puts it: "In a numeric sort, 9 comes before 80, but because numbers are converted to strings, '80' comes before '9' in the Unicode order."

```js
const array = [1, 30, 4, 21, 100000];
array.sort();
console.log(array);
// [1, 100000, 21, 30, 4]

const numberArray = [40, 1, 5, 200];
numberArray.sort();                 // [1, 200, 40, 5]
numberArray.sort(compareNumbers);   // [1, 5, 40, 200]

function compareNumbers(a, b) {
  return a - b;
}
```

For numeric ordering, pass a compare function such as `(a, b) => a - b`. The MDN page notes this works as long as the array doesn't contain `NaN`.

